<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles II of Naples</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1254">1254</a>–<a href="/years/1309">1309</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Charles the Lame, Charles of Salerno · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Capetian House of Anjou</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles II, called the Lame, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> from 1285 to 1309. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles I of Anjou</a>, he was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-of-lauria">Roger of Lauria</a> in a naval battle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Bay of Naples</a> in 1284 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">War of the Sicilian Vespers</a> and held by the Aragonese until 1288. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-caltabellotta">Peace of Caltabellotta</a> of 1302 left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to the Aragonese, and he governed the mainland kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-naples.jpg" alt="Charles II of Naples" width="204" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en:Cristoforo Orimina · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_2_of_Naples.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles I of Anjou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatrice-of-provence">Beatrice of Provence</a>, and the sole heir to his father's wide dominion, which included Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forcalquier">Forcalquier</a>, Anjou and Maine, and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. In 1272 his father made him Prince of Salerno. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a>, the uprising against Angevin rule that began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> on 30 March 1282, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III of Aragon</a> was declared king of the island on 4 September 1282. In 1283 Charles I made his son regent of the mainland territories. Charles held a general assembly that abolished unpopular taxes and confirmed the liberties of nobles and clergy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Against his father's orders, Charles sailed against the Aragonese on Nisida on 5 June 1284, and the Aragonese galleys surrounded and defeated his fleet. He was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>, where the crowd demanded his execution; to save his life Constance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> imprisoned him at Cefalù. His father died on 7 January 1285 while he was still a prisoner. By the treaty of Canfranc in October 1288 Charles was released for a ransom of 50,000 marks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, leaving his sons as hostages. Pope Nicholas IV crowned him king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rieti">Rieti</a> on Whit Sunday 1289. Charles ordered the expulsion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Anjou and Maine, forced many Jews of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to convert and introduced the Inquisition in the Regno.</p><p>Treaties at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senlis">Senlis</a> in 1290 and Brignoles in 1291 made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Aragon, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>, his eldest son, was granted a claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> by the pope in 1292. Frederick was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> on 12 December 1295 after the Sicilians refused the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anagni">Anagni</a>, and the long war ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-caltabellotta">Peace of Caltabellotta</a> in 1302, in which Charles gave up all rights to Sicily and agreed to the marriage of his daughter Eleanor to King Frederick.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles spent his last years quietly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, which he improved and embellished. He died there in May 1309 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-king-of-naples">Robert the Wise</a>, while his eldest grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary">Charles I of Hungary</a> was excluded from the Neapolitan succession. With his wife Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, daughter of Stephen V of Hungary, he had fourteen children. They included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Saint Louis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-of-taranto">Philip I of Taranto</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles II of Naples” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…won a series of victories against the Angevins after Peter III took Sicily in 1282: off Malta in 1283, in the Bay of Naples in 1284, where <b>Charles of Salerno</b> was captured, and at Les Formigues in 1285, which wrecked the French invasion known as the Aragonese Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The War of the Sicilian Vespers lasted twenty years: Charles's son <b>Charles II</b>, captured at sea by the Aragonese admiral Roger of Lauria in 1284, became king on his father's death in 1285 while still a prisoner, and the Peace of Caltabellotta in 1302 confirmed the division.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pope Boniface VIII, James II of Aragon, Philip IV of France, <b>Charles II of Naples</b> and James II of Majorca made an accord at Anagni confirming the Treaty of Tarascon of 1291.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-20">20 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the War of the Sicilian Vespers, an Aragonese–Sicilian fleet under Roger of Lauria defeated an Angevin fleet in the Gulf of Naples and captured its commander, Prince <b>Charles of Salerno</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-5">5 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1289" aria-label="Naples on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4899 706 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles II of Naples"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5349 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5349 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1289">What was here in 1289? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
